Dear Miaplaza Engineering Hiring Team,

I am applying for the hands-on Engineering Manager role. Two things in your advert made the decision for
me: the insistence that an EM stays in the code roughly half the time, and the pedagogical premise that
you cannot teach what you do not see. I have worked under that premise for a decade without having a name
for it.

On the technical side: I run a thirteen-host Linux fleet declared in one repository. Every machine
converges to the declaration, a change passes the same gate in CI and on the host, and rollback is a
pointer move. I do the bare metal myself — PXE and iVentoy through first configuration to rebuild — and I
read state from a ledger rather than a dashboard: job status, generation age, disk pressure and PSI every
fifteen minutes, paging on anything other than ok. That record is the same one an incident review needs,
which is why feedback here is specific rather than atmospheric. Go, Python and Bash are how I turn a
repeated manual step into something the machine does once.

Where I fall short, I would rather say it here. I have led people — two frac crews at Halliburton, safety
training owned end to end — but I have not managed software engineers with performance reviews and SMART
goals, and I have not run Kubernetes or GCP in production. Your two-month onboarding as a senior engineer
is, honestly, part of the appeal: it is the only management role I have seen that budgets time for the
manager to earn the team's technical respect before asking for it.

What I would bring to four engineers: a bias toward making the right action the easy one — written
runbooks, gates instead of reminders, infrastructure declared so that a newcomer can change it safely —
and a habit of measuring whether the platform actually lowered anyone's daily burden. Your goal of
consolidating and shrinking the stack is the kind of decision I have made repeatedly on my own systems,
always by asking what stops being necessary.

On logistics: 100% remote EMEA suits me; I work from Gutach im Breisgau and am happy to travel for team
onboarding. My salary expectation is EUR 110,000, and I understand placement also depends on regional
bands. I am a US citizen with German permanent residence and need no sponsorship.

Yours sincerely,
#v(0.5cm)
Matt Ji
